window.CARDS = [{"deck":"Tools → Purpose","front":"RegRipper","back":"Parses OFFLINE Windows registry hives via plugins (UserAssist, ShellBags, USBSTOR, Run keys)."},{"deck":"Tools → Purpose","front":"Volatility / Volatility3","back":"Memory (RAM) image analysis framework. Plugins: pslist, psscan, psxview, malfind, dlllist, netscan, hivelist.\nthe courseware places it under MALWARE ANALYSIS memory-dumping tools (with Belkasoft Live RAM Capturer) — their Module 06 'Memory Analysis Tools' slide leads with Velociraptor and WinDbg instead."},{"deck":"Tools → Purpose","front":"FTK Imager","back":"Acquisition + preview. Images in raw/SMART/E01/AFF, computes MD5/SHA-1 during acquisition, captures RAM, read-only preview."},{"deck":"Tools → Purpose","front":"Autopsy / The Sleuth Kit","back":"Open-source analysis platform. TSK CLI tools: fls, icat, mmls, fsstat, istat, blkls."},{"deck":"Tools → Purpose","front":"Wireshark","back":"Packet capture and protocol analysis. Manual stream following, display filters."},{"deck":"Tools → Purpose","front":"NetworkMiner","back":"Network forensic analysis tool — AUTOMATICALLY carves files, images, credentials and host profiles from PCAP."},{"deck":"Tools → Purpose","front":"Foremost / Scalpel / PhotoRec","back":"File carving by header/footer signature — works WITHOUT file system metadata."},{"deck":"Tools → Purpose","front":"Hashcat","back":"GPU-accelerated password recovery. Dictionary, rule, mask, brute-force, hybrid attacks."},{"deck":"Tools → Purpose","front":"John the Ripper","back":"CPU-oriented password cracker (GPU in jumbo build). Auto-detects hash types."},{"deck":"Tools → Purpose","front":"IDA Pro / Ghidra","back":"Disassemblers/decompilers — STATIC malware analysis."},{"deck":"Tools → Purpose","front":"PEiD / Detect It Easy","back":"Packer and compiler identification — STATIC analysis of PE files."},{"deck":"Tools → Purpose","front":"Process Monitor (ProcMon)","back":"DYNAMIC analysis — real-time file, registry, process and thread activity."},{"deck":"Tools → Purpose","front":"Regshot","back":"DYNAMIC analysis — before/after registry snapshot diff."},{"deck":"Tools → Purpose","front":"Cuckoo Sandbox","back":"Automated DYNAMIC malware analysis sandbox."},{"deck":"Tools → Purpose","front":"INetSim / FakeNet-NG","back":"Simulated internet services for sandbox analysis — captures callbacks without reaching real C2."},{"deck":"Tools → Purpose","front":"ExifTool","back":"Reads/writes metadata (EXIF, IPTC, XMP) in images, documents, video. Note: it can WRITE, so EXIF is forgeable."},{"deck":"Tools → Purpose","front":"StegDetect / StegExpose","back":"Steganalysis — statistical detection of hidden payloads in carrier files."},{"deck":"Tools → Purpose","front":"hdparm","back":"Linux ATA utility — detects and temporarily removes HPA/DCO hidden areas."},{"deck":"Tools → Purpose","front":"Arsenal Image Mounter","back":"Mounts forensic images as real disks, including Volume Shadow Copy access."},{"deck":"Tools → Purpose","front":"ShadowExplorer / vssadmin","back":"Enumerate and browse Volume Shadow Copy snapshots."},{"deck":"Zimmerman Tools","front":"MFTECmd","back":"Parses NTFS $MFT, $J (UsnJrnl), $Boot, $SDS."},{"deck":"Zimmerman Tools","front":"PECmd","back":"Parses Prefetch (.pf) files — execution evidence, run count, timestamps."},{"deck":"Zimmerman Tools","front":"LECmd","back":"Parses LNK shortcut files."},{"deck":"Zimmerman Tools","front":"JLECmd","back":"Parses Jump Lists (automatic + custom destinations)."},{"deck":"Zimmerman Tools","front":"SBECmd","back":"Parses ShellBags (folder navigation history)."},{"deck":"Zimmerman Tools","front":"AmcacheParser","back":"Parses Amcache.hve — installed/executed binaries with SHA-1 hashes."},{"deck":"Zimmerman Tools","front":"RECmd","back":"Command-line registry hive parser with batch plugin support."},{"deck":"Zimmerman Tools","front":"Timeline Explorer","back":"CSV/timeline viewer for reviewing parsed artifact output."},{"deck":"Windows Artifacts","front":"Prefetch — location and what it proves","back":"C:\\Windows\\Prefetch\\*.pf — proves EXECUTION: run count, first/last run times, referenced files."},{"deck":"Windows Artifacts","front":"ShimCache (AppCompatCache) — key caveat","back":"HKLM\\SYSTEM\\CurrentControlSet\\Control\\Session Manager\\AppCompatCache. Presence does NOT prove execution — can be created by directory enumeration."},{"deck":"Windows Artifacts","front":"Amcache.hve","back":"C:\\Windows\\AppCompat\\Programs\\Amcache.hve — installed/executed programs, full paths, SHA-1 hashes."},{"deck":"Windows Artifacts","front":"UserAssist","back":"NTUSER.DAT\\Software\\Microsoft\\Windows\\CurrentVersion\\Explorer\\UserAssist — GUI-launched programs, run count, last run. ROT13-encoded names."},{"deck":"Windows Artifacts","front":"ShellBags","back":"Registry keys storing Windows Explorer configuration — size, view mode, window position, and the last time a folder was viewed. Prove a user accessed a directory EVEN IF IT WAS DELETED OR THE DRIVE WAS FORMATTED. See the 'ShellBags — Windows 11 registry locations' card for exact paths."},{"deck":"Windows Artifacts","front":"USB device history","back":"SYSTEM\\CurrentControlSet\\Enum\\USBSTOR (vendor/product/serial) + C:\\Windows\\INF\\setupapi.dev.log (first connect time)."},{"deck":"Windows Artifacts","front":"Registry hive file locations","back":"SAM, SECURITY, SOFTWARE, SYSTEM → C:\\Windows\\System32\\config\\. NTUSER.DAT → C:\\Users\\<user>\\. UsrClass.dat → ...\\AppData\\Local\\Microsoft\\Windows\\."},{"deck":"Windows Artifacts","front":"Recycle Bin ($Recycle.Bin)","back":"$I file = original path, deletion timestamp, size. $R file = the actual recovered content."},{"deck":"Windows Artifacts","front":"Memory-on-disk artifacts","back":"pagefile.sys (paged memory), hiberfil.sys (compressed RAM image at hibernation), swapfile.sys (modern app suspend), MEMORY.DMP (crash dump)."},{"deck":"Windows Artifacts","front":"NTFS timestamp attributes","back":"$STANDARD_INFORMATION (user-modifiable) vs $FILE_NAME (kernel-set). Mismatch = TIMESTOMPING. 4 timestamps each = 8 total."},{"deck":"Windows Artifacts","front":"NTFS journals","back":"$LogFile (transaction journal, metadata rollback) and $UsnJrnl:$J (change journal — file create/delete/rename history)."},{"deck":"Windows Artifacts","front":"Alternate Data Streams (ADS)","back":"NTFS feature allowing hidden data attached to a file: file.txt:hidden.exe. Enumerate with dir /R or streams.exe."},{"deck":"Windows Artifacts","front":"Volume Shadow Copies (VSS)","back":"Point-in-time snapshots — often the ONLY surviving copy of a file deleted weeks ago."},{"deck":"Windows Artifacts","front":"Jump Lists","back":"AutomaticDestinations-ms / CustomDestinations-ms — recently and frequently opened files per application."},{"deck":"Windows Event IDs","front":"4624","back":"Successful logon. Check Logon Type."},{"deck":"Windows Event IDs","front":"4625","back":"Failed logon."},{"deck":"Windows Event IDs","front":"4634 / 4647","back":"Logoff / user-initiated logoff."},{"deck":"Windows Event IDs","front":"4648","back":"Logon using EXPLICIT credentials — strong lateral movement indicator."},{"deck":"Windows Event IDs","front":"4688 / 4689","back":"Process creation / process termination."},{"deck":"Windows Event IDs","front":"4720 / 4726","back":"User account created / deleted."},{"deck":"Windows Event IDs","front":"4672","back":"Special privileges assigned to new logon (admin-equivalent)."},{"deck":"Windows Event IDs","front":"1102","back":"Security audit log was CLEARED — anti-forensics indicator."},{"deck":"Windows Event IDs","front":"4104 / 4103","back":"PowerShell Script Block Logging (records DEOBFUSCATED script) / Module Logging."},{"deck":"Windows Event IDs","front":"Logon Types 2 / 3 / 4 / 5 / 10","back":"2 = Interactive, 3 = Network, 4 = Batch, 5 = Service, 10 = RemoteInteractive (RDP)."},{"deck":"File Signatures","front":"JPEG","back":"Header FF D8 FF — Footer FF D9"},{"deck":"File Signatures","front":"PNG","back":"89 50 4E 47 0D 0A 1A 0A (\\x89PNG)"},{"deck":"File Signatures","front":"GIF","back":"47 49 46 38 (GIF8) — GIF87a or GIF89a"},{"deck":"File Signatures","front":"PDF","back":"25 50 44 46 (%PDF) — Footer %%EOF"},{"deck":"File Signatures","front":"ZIP / DOCX / XLSX / PPTX / JAR / APK","back":"50 4B 03 04 (PK..) — all are ZIP containers"},{"deck":"File Signatures","front":"Windows PE executable","back":"4D 5A (MZ) at offset 0; 'PE\\0\\0' (50 45 00 00) at the offset stored in 0x3C"},{"deck":"File Signatures","front":"RAR","back":"52 61 72 21 1A 07 (Rar!)"},{"deck":"File Signatures","front":"ELF (Linux executable)","back":"7F 45 4C 46 (\\x7FELF)"},{"deck":"File Signatures","front":"GZIP","back":"1F 8B 08"},{"deck":"File Signatures","front":"Legacy MS Office (DOC/XLS/PPT)","back":"D0 CF 11 E0 A1 B1 1A E1 (OLE compound file)"},{"deck":"Linux / Mac Artifacts","front":"/var/log/wtmp","back":"Binary log of SUCCESSFUL logins/logouts. Read with: last"},{"deck":"Linux / Mac Artifacts","front":"/var/log/btmp","back":"BAD (failed) login attempts. Read with: lastb"},{"deck":"Linux / Mac Artifacts","front":"/var/run/utmp","back":"Currently logged-in users. Read with: who / w"},{"deck":"Linux / Mac Artifacts","front":"/var/log/auth.log or /var/log/secure","back":"Authentication events, sudo usage, SSH activity. (Debian: auth.log, RHEL: secure)"},{"deck":"Linux / Mac Artifacts","front":"~/.bash_history","back":"Shell command history. Note: can be cleared or redirected to /dev/null — absence is itself suspicious."},{"deck":"Linux / Mac Artifacts","front":"/proc/<PID>/exe","back":"Symlink to the running binary — recovers executables DELETED from disk while still running."},{"deck":"Linux / Mac Artifacts","front":"Linux persistence locations","back":"crontab (/etc/crontab, /var/spool/cron), systemd units, /etc/rc.local, ~/.bashrc, LD_PRELOAD, SSH authorized_keys."},{"deck":"Linux / Mac Artifacts","front":"macOS download provenance","back":"com.apple.metadata:kMDItemWhereFroms (extended attribute) + LaunchServices QuarantineEventsV2 SQLite DB."},{"deck":"Linux / Mac Artifacts","front":"macOS persistence","back":"LaunchAgents (~/Library, /Library) and LaunchDaemons (/Library/LaunchDaemons) — .plist files."},{"deck":"Linux / Mac Artifacts","front":"macOS .DS_Store","back":"Folder display settings — can prove which files WERE in a folder even after deletion."},{"deck":"Linux / Mac Artifacts","front":"macOS FSEvents","back":"/.fseventsd — file system change history, valuable timeline source."},{"deck":"File Systems","front":"NTFS MFT record size and residency","back":"Typically 1024 bytes. Small files are stored RESIDENT inside the MFT record — no cluster allocated."},{"deck":"File Systems","front":"File slack — definition and parts","back":"Allocated space minus logical file size. RAM slack (to end of final sector) + drive slack (remaining sectors). May hold prior file data."},{"deck":"File Systems","front":"What deletion actually does (NTFS)","back":"MFT record flagged unallocated + clusters marked free in $Bitmap. CONTENT REMAINS until overwritten."},{"deck":"File Systems","front":"MBR vs GPT","back":"MBR: sector 0, 4 primary partitions, 2 TB limit. GPT: protective MBR + primary AND backup headers with CRC32 at both ends of disk."},{"deck":"File Systems","front":"FAT32 limits","back":"4 GB max file size, 32 GB practical volume limit (Windows format), minimal metadata, no journaling."},{"deck":"File Systems","front":"HPA and DCO","back":"ATA hidden areas. Symptom: reported capacity < manufacturer spec. Detect/remove with hdparm or an HPA/DCO-aware imager."},{"deck":"File Systems","front":"ext3/ext4 key structures","back":"Superblock, inode table, journal. Inodes hold metadata + pointers; filenames live in directory entries, not inodes."},{"deck":"Acquisition","front":"Order of volatility (full sequence)","back":"CPU registers/cache → routing table, ARP cache, process table, kernel stats, RAM → temporary file systems → disk → remote logging/monitoring → physical config & network topology → archival media."},{"deck":"Acquisition","front":"Physical vs logical acquisition","back":"Physical = bit-for-bit ALL sectors (unallocated, slack, deleted, HPA/DCO). Logical = only active files visible to the file system."},{"deck":"Acquisition","front":"E01 vs raw dd","back":"E01/Ex01: embedded case metadata, per-block CRC, stored hashes, compression. Raw dd: pure bitstream, no metadata — hashes stored separately."},{"deck":"Acquisition","front":"Hardware vs software write blocker","back":"Hardware: inline on the bus, filters write commands, OS-independent, preferred. Software: OS-level, depends on correct configuration."},{"deck":"Acquisition","front":"SSD forensic caveat","back":"TRIM + garbage collection let the controller zero deleted blocks AUTONOMOUSLY — a write blocker does NOT stop it. Acquire promptly."},{"deck":"Acquisition","front":"RAID 5 acquisition","back":"Striped with DISTRIBUTED parity (no dedicated parity disk). Must image ALL members and reconstruct with correct stripe size, disk order, parity rotation."},{"deck":"Acquisition","front":"Full-disk encryption rule","back":"If powered on and UNLOCKED: capture RAM (keys) and image the decrypted volume LIVE. Never pull the plug — you lose access."},{"deck":"Acquisition","front":"Live, unlocked phone rule","back":"Do NOT power off. Isolate in a Faraday bag/shielded container WITH power to prevent remote wipe and avoid re-locking."},{"deck":"Acquisition","front":"Evidence packaging","back":"Drives → ANTISTATIC bags (not standard plastic). Phones/wireless → Faraday/shielded. Label, seal, document."},{"deck":"Network Forensics","front":"NetFlow / IPFIX","back":"Connection METADATA (src/dst IP+port, protocol, bytes, packets, timestamps) — no payload, so long retention is practical."},{"deck":"Network Forensics","front":"DNS tunneling indicators","back":"Long high-entropy subdomain labels, regular query intervals, single authoritative parent domain, high query volume."},{"deck":"Network Forensics","front":"Beaconing","back":"Regular-interval outbound connections to the same destination = automated C2. Look for low jitter and consistent byte counts."},{"deck":"Network Forensics","front":"IIS log location","back":"C:\\inetpub\\logs\\LogFiles\\W3SVC<n>\\ — W3C format: c-ip, cs-method, cs-uri-stem, cs-uri-query, sc-status."},{"deck":"Network Forensics","front":"Apache log location","back":"/var/log/apache2/access.log (Debian) or /var/log/httpd/access_log (RHEL). Also error.log."},{"deck":"Network Forensics","front":"Web log limitation","back":"GET query strings ARE logged; POST BODIES are NOT logged by default — injection via POST may be invisible in access logs."},{"deck":"Malware Forensics","front":"Static vs dynamic analysis","back":"Static = examine WITHOUT running (PEiD, IDA/Ghidra, strings, imports). Dynamic = run in isolation and observe (ProcMon, Regshot, Wireshark, Cuckoo)."},{"deck":"Malware Forensics","front":"Packing indicators","back":"Section entropy > ~7.0, few readable strings, minimal imports dominated by LoadLibrary/GetProcAddress."},{"deck":"Malware Forensics","front":"pslist vs psscan","back":"pslist walks the EPROCESS linked list (defeated by DKOM unlinking). psscan scans for pool tag signatures — FINDS hidden processes. psxview compares both."},{"deck":"Malware Forensics","front":"Windows persistence locations","back":"Run/RunOnce keys, Scheduled Tasks, Services, WMI event subscriptions (most often missed), DLL search-order hijack, AppInit_DLLs, Winlogon shell."},{"deck":"Malware Forensics","front":"Fileless malware evidence","back":"PowerShell 4104 (deobfuscated script blocks), 4103, transcripts, memory capture, WMI repository, encoded payloads in registry values."},{"deck":"Mobile Forensics","front":"IMEI vs IMSI vs ICCID","back":"IMEI = handset EQUIPMENT. IMSI = SUBSCRIBER (on SIM). ICCID = the SIM CARD's own serial number."},{"deck":"Mobile Forensics","front":"Mobile extraction hierarchy","back":"Manual (photograph screens) → Logical (allocated files/APIs) → File System → Physical (raw flash, reaches unallocated/deleted)."},{"deck":"Mobile Forensics","front":"Android app data location","back":"/data/data/<package>/databases/ — SQLite. Deleted records persist in -wal, -shm and freelist pages."},{"deck":"Mobile Forensics","front":"iOS backup artifacts","back":"Manifest.db maps hashed filenames to real paths; app data in SQLite; keychain holds credentials."},{"deck":"Cloud Forensics","front":"AWS CloudTrail vs CloudWatch vs Config","back":"CloudTrail = API audit (WHO did WHAT, when, from where). CloudWatch = metrics/app logs. Config = resource configuration state history."},{"deck":"Cloud Forensics","front":"Forensic visibility by service model","back":"IaaS (most access: OS, volumes, memory) → PaaS (app + data) → SaaS (least: only provider-exposed logs and exports)."},{"deck":"Cloud Forensics","front":"Top cloud forensic challenges","back":"Jurisdiction/data location, multi-tenancy, provider dependence, short log retention, volatile ephemeral instances, limited physical access."},{"deck":"Cloud Forensics","front":"Cross-border evidence mechanisms","back":"MLAT (traditional, slow) or CLOUD Act (compels US providers based on CONTROL, not storage location)."},{"deck":"Email Forensics","front":"Reading Received: headers","back":"Read BOTTOM-UP — each hop PREPENDS its line, so the bottom-most is closest to the true origin."},{"deck":"Email Forensics","front":"Return-Path vs From","back":"Return-Path = envelope sender (MAIL FROM). From = displayed header. Mismatch + spf=fail = likely spoofing."},{"deck":"Email Forensics","front":"SPF / DKIM / DMARC","back":"SPF: is the sending IP authorized for the domain? DKIM: cryptographic signature over headers/body. DMARC: policy tying SPF/DKIM to the From domain + reporting."},{"deck":"Email Forensics","front":"Mail store formats","back":"PST/OST (Outlook), MBOX (single file, many messages), EML (single message), Maildir (one file per message)."},{"deck":"Anti-forensics","front":"Timestomping detection","back":"Compare $STANDARD_INFORMATION against $FILE_NAME timestamps in the $MFT — most tools alter only $SI."},{"deck":"Anti-forensics","front":"What survives free-space wiping","back":"$MFT resident data, slack in allocated files, Volume Shadow Copies, pagefile/hiberfil, $LogFile/$UsnJrnl entries — plus the wiper's own Prefetch entry (proves intent)."},{"deck":"Anti-forensics","front":"Tor artifacts after uninstall","back":"Prefetch/Amcache/ShimCache entries, registry MRU/UserAssist, .onion strings in pagefile/hiberfil, torrc/state remnants. NOTE: .onion is NOT resolved by DNS."},{"deck":"Anti-forensics","front":"Steganography vs steganalysis","back":"Steganography HIDES data in a carrier (LSB substitution, DCT coefficients). Steganalysis DETECTS it (chi-square, RS analysis)."},{"deck":"Legal & Standards","front":"Daubert factors","back":"Testability/falsifiability, peer review and publication, known/potential ERROR RATE, controlling standards, general acceptance."},{"deck":"Legal & Standards","front":"Frye standard","back":"General acceptance in the relevant scientific community — ONLY. Older and narrower than Daubert."},{"deck":"Legal & Standards","front":"Best evidence rule (for ESI)","back":"Satisfied by an accurate duplicate — a verified forensic image qualifies. It is about proving CONTENT, not producing the physical object."},{"deck":"Legal & Standards","front":"ACPO / NPCC principles","back":"1: Don't change data. 2: If you must access originals, be COMPETENT and able to JUSTIFY it. 3: Keep an audit trail. 4: Case officer is responsible for compliance."},{"deck":"Legal & Standards","front":"ISO/IEC 27037 / 27041 / 27042 / 27043","back":"27037: identify, collect, acquire, preserve. 27041: assuring method suitability. 27042: analysis and interpretation. 27043: overall investigation principles."},{"deck":"Legal & Standards","front":"GDPR breach notification","back":"Supervisory authority: without undue delay, where feasible within 72 HOURS OF AWARENESS. Data subjects: without undue delay if HIGH RISK."},{"deck":"Legal & Standards","front":"Evidence characteristics (5)","back":"Admissible, Authentic, Complete, Reliable, Believable/Understandable."},{"deck":"Legal & Standards","front":"Locard's exchange principle (digital)","back":"Every contact leaves a trace — attacker leaves artifacts on victim systems AND carries traces back on their own."},{"deck":"Legal & Standards","front":"Reconstruction types","back":"Temporal (when/order), Relational (who/what relates to what), Functional (was it even POSSIBLE?)."},{"deck":"Legal & Standards","front":"Attribution limit","back":"Artifacts attribute activity to an ACCOUNT, never directly to a PERSON. Overstating this is a career-damaging testimony error."},{"deck":"Key Enumerations","front":"Rules of Evidence (the courseware list)","back":"Understandable · Admissible · Authentic · Reliable · Complete · Materiality.\nNOTE: the text says 'five basic rules' but enumerates SIX. Materiality is the one people drop."},{"deck":"Key Enumerations","front":"Types of Digital Evidence (2 categories)","back":"VOLATILE — lost when powered off: system time, logged-on users, open files, network info, process info, process-to-port mapping, process memory, clipboard, service/driver info, command history.\nNON-VOLATILE — on secondary storage: hidden files, slack space, swap file, index.dat, unallocated clusters, unused/hidden partitions, registry settings, event logs."},{"deck":"Key Enumerations","front":"Sources of Potential Evidence (3 categories)","back":"USER-CREATED: address books, database files, media, documents, bookmarks/favorites, emails + cloud storage, hidden partitions.\nUSER-PROTECTED: compressed, misnamed, encrypted, password-protected, hidden files, steganography, blockchain ledgers.\nCOMPUTER-CREATED: backup, log, configuration, printer spool, cookies, swap, system, history, temporary, registry files."},{"deck":"Key Enumerations","front":"Types vs Sources — don't confuse","back":"TYPES of digital evidence = volatile / non-volatile.\nSOURCES of potential evidence = user-created / user-protected / computer-created.\nTwo different taxonomies, both examinable."},{"deck":"Key Enumerations","front":"Roles of Digital Evidence","back":"Proof of an act · Corroborative and contradictory · Linking · Exculpatory · Lead information · Contextual · Timeline construction · Identity verification/motivation clarification · Supporting prosecution or defense · Policy and compliance verification."},{"deck":"Key Enumerations","front":"Best Evidence Rule (the courseware framing)","back":"The court allows the ORIGINAL of a document, photograph, or recording at trial rather than a copy — the original is the best evidence. A duplicate is accepted only if the court finds the party's reasons for submitting it GENUINE (original destroyed, lost, or held by a third party) and a witness confirms it is an actual copy."},{"deck":"Key Enumerations","front":"Computer Forensics vs eDiscovery","back":"eDiscovery: Federal Rules of CIVIL Procedure · mainly civil cases · EXISTING data only · high standards not required.\nComputer forensics: Federal Rules of CRIMINAL Procedure · mainly criminal cases · carving/recovery of DELETED files · strictly documented procedure required."},{"deck":"Key Enumerations","front":"Obscure evidence sources in the courseware table","back":"Scanner → marks on the GLASS. Dongle → connection records, IMEI and SIM info, cached data, file artifacts. Printer → usage logs, time/date, network identity. Copier → documents, user usage logs, time/date stamps. Answering machine → deleted messages, last calls, saved numbers."},{"deck":"Legal & Standards","front":"FRE 803 vs 804","back":"803 = hearsay exceptions where declarant availability is IMMATERIAL (present sense impression, excited utterance, medical diagnosis, recorded recollection, records of regularly conducted activity, absence of entry, public records, vital statistics).\n804 = declarant UNAVAILABLE (former testimony, statement under belief of impending death, statement against interest, statement of personal/family history)."},{"deck":"Legal & Standards","front":"FRE rules in the courseware table","back":"102 Purpose · 103 Rulings on Evidence · 104 Preliminary Questions · 105 Limited Admissibility · 801 Hearsay Rule + Statements That Are Not Hearsay · 803 Exceptions (availability immaterial) · 804 Exceptions (declarant unavailable) · 1001 Definitions (writing, recording, photograph, original, duplicate)."},{"deck":"Legal & Standards","front":"FRE 1001 — why it matters","back":"Defines 'duplicate' as a counterpart produced by a mechanical, photographic, chemical, electronic, or other equivalent process that ACCURATELY REPRODUCES the original. This is the definition that makes forensic imaging legally workable."},{"deck":"Legal & Standards","front":"Course structure (how the courseware is organised)","back":"15 modules → each module split into LO#01, LO#02 … (Learning Objectives) → each LO opens with a title slide listing its sub-topics → each sub-topic = a slide graphic followed by prose elaboration. Exam items are drawn heavily from the SLIDE graphics: numbered lists, comparison tables, and category boxes."},{"deck":"Named Tools","front":"Digital Forensic Imaging Tools (M04 p631)","back":"OSFClone (lead — self-booting, clones raw disk images independently of installed OS, supports AFF and EWF, preserves fragmentation/slack/unused sectors/undeleted records)\n+ Talon Ultimate · Falcon-NEO · PALADIN · OpenText EnCase Forensic · Belkasoft X"},{"deck":"Named Tools","front":"Memory Analysis Tools (M06 p863)","back":"Velociraptor (lead — endpoint monitoring/DFIR platform, uses Velociraptor Query Language VQL; pslist() for process info; SELECT * FROM pslist() WHERE TokenIsElevated finds elevated shells)\n+ WinDbg\nNOTE: the courseware leads with Velociraptor here, NOT Volatility."},{"deck":"Named Tools","front":"Windows Forensics Tools (M06 p1101)","back":"OSForensics (lead — identifies suspicious files/activities via hash matching, drive signature comparison, analyses emails, memory, binary data; file listing / thumbnail / timeline views)\n+ Cellebrite Digital Collector · Oxygen Forensic Detective · X-Ways Forensics · Registry Recon · OpenText EnCase Forensic"},{"deck":"Named Tools","front":"Password Cracking Tools (M05 p689)","back":"Passware Kit Forensic (lead — decrypts password-protected items, identifies 350+ file types, batch mode)\n+ John the Ripper · THC-Hydra · hashcat · Secure Shell Bruteforcer · DataProtectionDecryptor"},{"deck":"Named Tools","front":"Steganography Detection Tools (M05 p712)","back":"zsteg (lead — detects stego-hidden data in PNG and BMP files)\n+ StegoVeritas · Stegextract · StegoHunt MP · StegSpy · DeepSound"},{"deck":"Named Tools","front":"File Recovery Tools: Windows (M05 p661)","back":"EaseUS Data Recovery Wizard (lead — recovers from deletion, formatting, partition loss, unbootable/crashed systems, virus attack; specialises in disk partition recovery)\n+ DiskDigger · Handy Recovery · Active@ File Recovery · Stellar Data Recovery · PhotoRec"},{"deck":"Named Tools","front":"Sniffing Tools (M08 p1307)","back":"tcpdump (lead — CLI packet analyzer; -w writes to file, -r reads from file, -c stops after N packets; reports packets 'captured' and 'received by filter')\n+ Wireshark (captures live traffic from Ethernet, IEEE 802.11, PPP/HDLC, ATM, Bluetooth, USB, Token Ring, Frame Relay, FDDI)"},{"deck":"Named Tools","front":"IoT Forensics Tools (M15 p2304)","back":"MD-NEXT (lead — drones, smart TVs, wearables, IoT devices, USIM cards, SD cards, JTAG boards, chip-off memory; supports BOTH physical and logical extraction for Android, iOS, Windows, Tizen)\n+ IoT Inspector · Magnet AXIOM · NetOdin3"},{"deck":"Named Tools","front":"Malware Analysis — HARDWARE tools (M09 p1435)","back":"1. Ready-to-use jump kit with connectors to acquire and back up data from the compromised system\n2. Storage media to store the acquired data and backup\n3. Write-protected device to prevent modification during acquisition\n4. A system installed with a virtual client to run the sandbox"},{"deck":"Named Tools","front":"Malware Analysis — SOFTWARE tools by job (M09 p1435)","back":"Virtualization: VirtualBox, VMware vSphere Hypervisor, Microsoft Hyper-V Server\nForensic image extraction: FTK Imager\nPE analysis: PEview, PEStudio, PEiD, FileAlyzer\nSnapshots: Regshot, Total Commander\nMemory dumping: Belkasoft Live RAM Capturer, Volatility Framework\nNetwork sniffing: Wireshark, tcpdump\nNetwork simulation: iNetSim\nProcess exploring/monitoring: Process Monitor, Process Explorer\nHex viewing: Hex Editor Neo, 010 Editor, Hexinator\nDebugging: OllyDbg, Ghidra, IDA Pro\nMalicious strings: Bintext, FLOSS, Hex Workshop\nDependencies: Dependency Walker"},{"deck":"Named Tools","front":"NTFS Journals — the two data streams (M03 p390)","back":"$UsnJrnl:$J — the actual journal entries of file and folder changes on the volume.\n$UsnJrnl:$MAX — the METADATA of $UsnJrnl.\n$J records: file/directory name, Sequence Number, file/directory attributes, USN Reason String, USN Reason Hex, Timestamp, USN reference number.\nTools: NTFS Journal Viewer, FTK Imager."},{"deck":"Named Tools","front":"Startup Program Monitoring Tool (M09 p1514)","back":"Autoruns for Windows — enumerates auto-starting locations (Run keys, services, scheduled tasks, drivers, codecs, browser helper objects)."},{"deck":"Named Tools","front":"Why your Tools score was low","back":"the courseware v11 leads with COMMERCIAL products, not the open-source DFIR stack. Where a practitioner reaches for Volatility, RegRipper, Autopsy or Eric Zimmerman's tools, the courseware names Velociraptor, OSForensics, Passware, EaseUS, Belkasoft, X-Ways, Cellebrite, Oxygen and OpenText EnCase. Learn BOTH — but the exam tests theirs."},{"deck":"Scene & Power-State Decisions","front":"Powered-ON computer — first steps","back":"Screen VIEWABLE → photograph the screen, document running programs, open files, data of evidentiary value.\nSCREENSAVER showing → move the mouse SLOWLY without pressing any mouse button, then photograph and document.\nPortable WAKES UP → record time and date, photograph, document all running programs."},{"deck":"Scene & Power-State Decisions","front":"When to PULL THE POWER CORD immediately","back":"Only when there is:\n· on-screen indication of data being overwritten or deleted, or\n· destructive processes observed running to obliterate data.\nPull from the BACK of the computer."},{"deck":"Scene & Power-State Decisions","front":"When NOT to disconnect power","back":"· Data of evidential value is visible on the display\n· Active programs or files in use — chatrooms, open text files, financial documents, instant messages\nCollect volatile data first; THEN pull the plug from the back."},{"deck":"Scene & Power-State Decisions","front":"Portable computer shutdown","back":"REMOVE THE BATTERY **and** unplug the power cord from the wall socket. Pulling only the mains lead leaves a laptop running on battery."},{"deck":"Scene & Power-State Decisions","front":"Powered-OFF computer — the rule","back":"If it is switched off, LEAVE IT OFF. Never power on to 'check something'.\nPhotograph the computer, connections, peripherals and surroundings before moving anything. Inspect for tampering, damage or self-destruction mechanisms. Check for notes and written passwords. Assign a unique evidence number. Document make, model, serial."},{"deck":"Scene & Power-State Decisions","front":"Blank display — monitor OFF vs monitor ON","back":"Monitor switched OFF, display blank → turn the MONITOR on, move the mouse slightly, observe the change from blank to another screen, note it, photograph.\nMonitor switched ON, display blank → move the mouse slightly; if the screen does NOT change, DO NOT PRESS ANY KEYS. Photograph."},{"deck":"Scene & Power-State Decisions","front":"Powered-off packaging sequence","back":"1. Remove the power supply cord from the back of the computer and the wall outlet or battery backup device; secure it\n2. Disconnect all wires and cables; secure them\n3. Check for removable media and secure if present\n4. Tag the evidence clearly; note all details in the search and seizure evidence log\n5. Document the chain of custody"},{"deck":"Scene & Power-State Decisions","front":"OS shutdown — the precondition","back":"Volatile data must be collected (or its collection awaited) BEFORE shutdown — it is deleted on shutdown and cannot be retrieved. Follow each OS's predefined procedure or data may be lost as hard drives crash."},{"deck":"Scene & Power-State Decisions","front":"OS shutdown — Windows / macOS / Linux","back":"WINDOWS: photograph screen · document running programs · Windows button → Power → Shut down · wait for full shutdown · unplug from wall socket.\nmacOS: record the time from the menu bar · Apple icon → Shut Down · DESELECT 'Reopen windows when logging back in'.\nLINUX: right-click Desktop → Open Terminal · 'shutdown now' or 'shutdown -h 0' · prefix sudo if admin rights requested."},{"deck":"Acquisition Decisions","front":"Rules of Thumb for Data Acquisition (6)","back":"Document every process · Consider a forensic methodology · Preserve original evidence · Verify hash values · Legal authorization · Maintain chain of custody."},{"deck":"Acquisition Decisions","front":"How many copies of the original medium?","back":"TWO, before starting the investigation:\n1. Working copy — used for analysis\n2. Library / control copy — stored for disclosure, or used if the working copy is corrupted."},{"deck":"Acquisition Decisions","front":"Acquisition layer model (Step 1)","back":"Application Layer + File System Layer → LOGICAL / SPARSE acquisition.\nPartition/Volume Layer + Disk Layer → FULL IMAGE.\nKey factors: size of suspect drive · time required to acquire the image · can the suspect drive be retained."},{"deck":"Acquisition Decisions","front":"Imaging tool MANDATORY requirements (Step 2)","back":"· Must not alter the original content\n· Must log I/O errors in an accessible, readable form (type and location)\n· Must compare source and destination and ALERT if destination is smaller\n· Must pass scientific and peer review — repeatable and verifiable by a third party\n· Must completely acquire all visible AND hidden data sectors\n· Must create a bitstream copy when there are no errors\n· Must create a QUALIFIED bitstream copy when I/O errors occur"},{"deck":"Acquisition Decisions","front":"Imaging tool OPTIONAL requirements (Step 2)","back":"· Compute the hash of the created image and compare with the original source\n· Divide the bitstream copy into blocks, hash each block, compare with source\n· Log one or more items on a disk file\n· Create a bitstream copy of individual partitions per user direction\n· Make the source disk partition table visible and record its contents\nNOTE: hash verification is OPTIONAL in the courseware's tool criteria — counter-intuitive but examinable."},{"deck":"Acquisition Decisions","front":"macOS — Single User Mode acquisition","back":"Boot: hold Command (⌘) + S until the UNIX console appears.\nMount external USB storage · identify target with 'diskutil list' or 'ls /dev/disk*' · note device identifier (e.g. /dev/disk0).\nImage: dd if=<target_drive> bs=4k conv=noerror,sync of=<destination_drive>"},{"deck":"Acquisition Decisions","front":"macOS — Target Disk Mode acquisition","back":"Requires TWO Macs with FireWire/Thunderbolt/USB interface, and Disk Arbitration DISABLED to prevent automatic mounting.\nSuspect off → hold T at start. Suspect on → Apple menu → System Preferences → Startup Disk → Target Disk Mode.\nAcquire physical image with dd or FTK Imager.\nFinish: EJECT the disk → press Power to shut down the suspect → THEN disconnect the cable."},{"deck":"Acquisition Decisions","front":"macOS non-volatile acquisition — the four routes","back":"Step 6.3 Single User Mode (dd) · Step 6.4 Target Disk Mode (dd / FTK Imager) · Step 6.5 Linux bootable USB · Step 6.6 Digital Collector (Cellebrite)."},{"deck":"Acquisition Decisions","front":"Linux volatile acquisition — four variants","back":"dd — local · dd + Netcat — remote · LiME — local · LiME + Netcat — remote.\nNetcat streaming avoids writing to the suspect's local disk."},{"deck":"Linux / Mac Artifacts","front":"APFS vs HFS+ — timestamps and epochs","back":"APFS: 64-bit unsigned, 1-NANOSECOND granularity, nanoseconds since 1970-01-01.\nHFS+: 1-SECOND granularity, seconds since 1904-01-01.\nAlso: APFS 64-bit inodes (9 quintillion files) vs HFS+ 32-bit (4 billion); APFS copy-on-write vs HFS+ journaling; APFS full-disk encryption vs HFS+ file/folder only."},{"deck":"Linux / Mac Artifacts","front":"APFS snapshots vs checkpoints","back":"SNAPSHOTS — read-only pictures of the file system showing device state at a point in time; the user can RESTORE from them via the filesystem API. The APFS analogue of Volume Shadow Copies.\nCHECKPOINTS — temporary state of a container, always storing the current state as the last state; volume + container metadata. Not restorable history."},{"deck":"Windows Artifacts","front":"Zone.Identifier ADS","back":"Alternate data stream marking a file as internet-downloaded — security zone and, in newer Windows, host and referrer URLs. The Windows counterpart to macOS kMDItemWhereFroms. Invisible to ordinary directory listings; stripped by many copy operations. The courseware covers dedicated tools for analysing it."},{"deck":"Windows Artifacts","front":"Prefetch — the exact offsets","back":"C:\\Windows\\Prefetch — ADMIN privileges required. Named APPNAME.EXE-<hash>.pf\nDWORD at OFFSET 144 = number of times the application was launched (run count)\nDWORD at OFFSET 120 = last time the application was run, in UTC\nControlled by HKLM\\SYSTEM\\ControlSet00x\\Control\\Session Manager\\Memory Management\\PrefetchParameters\nRecords data for up to the first 10 SECONDS after process start. Read with WinPrefetchView."},{"deck":"Windows Artifacts","front":"ShellBags — Windows 11 registry locations","back":"HKCU\\SOFTWARE\\Microsoft\\Windows\\Shell\\BagMRU\nHKCU\\SOFTWARE\\Microsoft\\Windows\\Shell\\Bags\nHKCU\\SOFTWARE\\Classes\\Local Settings\\Software\\Microsoft\\Windows\\Shell\\BagMRU\nHKCU\\SOFTWARE\\Classes\\Local Settings\\Software\\Microsoft\\Windows\\Shell\\Bags\nBagMRU = folders most recently accessed. Bags = view preferences (position, location, view mode, sort methods) under a 'Shell' subkey.\nProves access even if the folder was deleted OR THE DRIVE WAS FORMATTED."},{"deck":"Windows Artifacts","front":"Registry LastWrite time — what it applies to","back":"LastWrite is a property of registry KEYS, not values — comparable to last modified time for a file. Updated to the current LOCAL system time when the key or any of its values is created, altered, or deleted.\nA registry VALUE has no LastWrite of its own, but it can be INFERRED from the key's LastWrite. This makes the registry usable as a log file."},{"deck":"Windows Artifacts","front":"SRUM — System Resource Usage Monitor","back":"ESE database at C:\\Windows\\System32\\SRU\\SRUDB.dat. Starts automatically with the system.\nTracks resource usage by Windows and its applications, Windows push notifications, DATA USAGE and NETWORK CONNECTIVITY per application.\nTools: Velociraptor, Magnet AXIOM Examine, SRUM DUMP.\nUses: anomaly detection (high resource use by an unknown process) and IoC detection (a process communicating with an unknown IP).\nTask Manager's App history tab shows only a fraction of it."},{"deck":"Windows Artifacts","front":"USBSTOR key structure and timestamps","back":"HKLM\\SYSTEM\\CurrentControlSet\\Enum\\USBSTOR\\Disk&Ven_[Vendor]&Prod_[Product]&Rev_1.00\\[SerialNo]\nExport the device-specific key to a text file: the Last Write Time gives the FIRST INSERTION and LAST INSERTION timestamps.\nEnumerate with PowerShell:\nGet-ItemProperty -Path HKLM:\\SYSTEM\\CurrentControlSet\\Enum\\USBSTOR\\*\\*\\ | Select FriendlyName"},{"deck":"Windows Artifacts","front":"Zone.Identifier — PowerShell commands","back":"View all streams:  Get-Item -path <file> -stream *\nVerify a zone identifier ADS exists:  Get-Item <file_path> -Stream zone*\nDetail for the report:  Get-Item -path <file> -stream * | Select-Object -Property Filename, Stream, Length"},{"deck":"Named Tools","front":"Tools for Analyzing Jump Lists","back":"JumpListsView (nirsoft) — lead tool. Shows filename opened by the user, date/time of the file opening event, ID of the application used to open the file, and size/time/attributes per record. Exports to csv / tab-delimited / xml / html.\nAlso: Belkasoft Evidence Center · Link File & Jump List Parser."},{"deck":"Live Collection","front":"Volatile information — the courseware list","back":"System time · Logged-on user(s) · Open files · Network information · Network connections · Network status · Process information · Process-to-port mapping · Process memory · Mapped drives · Shares · Clipboard contents · Service/driver information · Command history."},{"deck":"Live Collection","front":"Why RAM is collected FIRST","back":"Per Locard's exchange principle, collect the contents of RAM at the BEGINNING of the investigation to minimise the impact of further steps on its integrity — the tools used to collect other volatile information will themselves modify memory contents."},{"deck":"Live Collection","front":"Non-volatile information — the courseware definition","back":"Remains unchanged after shutdown or power loss. Examples: emails, word processing documents, spreadsheets, and various 'deleted' files.\nUsually resides on the hard drive — including the SWAP FILE, SLACK SPACE and UNALLOCATED drive space. Other sources: external hard drives, USB storage drives, smartphone memory.\nAcquired during STATIC data acquisition (volatile is acquired during LIVE acquisition)."},{"deck":"Network Forensics","front":"Types of Network-based Evidence (4)","back":"FULL CONTENT DATA — the actual packets, stored as PCAP. No filtration. Tools: tcpdump, Wireshark.\nSESSION DATA — summary of a conversation between TWO network entities: destination IP and port, source IP and port, start time of the session, amount of information exchanged.\nALERT DATA — triggered by Snort IDS and Suricata, preprogrammed to examine traffic for IoCs. Signature-based, so beware FALSE POSITIVES.\nSTATISTICAL DATA — overall profile/summaries: timestamps of conversations, protocols and services used, average packet size, average packet rate."},{"deck":"Network Forensics","front":"Event Correlation Approaches (the courseware list)","back":"Graph-based (components as nodes, dependencies as edges; finds root cause) · Neural network-based · Codebook-based (groups all events, uses a codebook — FASTER than rule-based, fewer comparisons) · Rule-based (condition → action) · Field-based (compares events with single/multiple fields in normalized data) · Packet parameter/payload correlation · Route correlation (attack route) · Topology-based · Cross-domain (multiple distinct domains or sources) · Multivariate (multiple variables simultaneously) · Contextual (user identity, location, device type)."},{"deck":"Malware Forensics","front":"Malware Forensic Artifacts (the courseware table)","back":"FILE SYSTEM — executables in unknown locations, temp directories or unallocated disk space, files with DOUBLE EXTENSIONS or unexpected size changes\nREGISTRY — autostart keys with unauthorized changes, new/modified keys with unusual characteristics\nNETWORK TRAFFIC — traffic to known malicious IPs/domains, C2 patterns, irregular DNS requests to non-standard domains\nLOG FILE ANOMALIES — failed logins, security policy changes, IDS alerts, unusual application errors/crashes, unexpected starting/stopping of services\nPROCESS AND MEMORY — processes that do not usually run, familiar processes running from unusual locations, code injection into legitimate processes' memory, abnormal CPU/memory\nEMAIL — malicious attachments, large volumes of outgoing spam\nCRYPTOGRAPHIC — encrypted files and communications using SELF-SIGNED or UNTRUSTED SSL/TLS certificates\nAUTHENTICATION RECORDS — logins at unusual times or from unexpected locations, multiple account lockouts\nBROWSER — unauthorized extensions/plugins, unknown changes to browser settings"},{"deck":"Malware Forensics","front":"Static vs Dynamic — the courseware's alternative names","back":"STATIC = CODE ANALYSIS. Going through the executable binary code WITHOUT executing it. Collects technical pointers/simple signatures: filename, MD5 checksums or hashes, file type, file size.\nDYNAMIC = BEHAVIORAL ANALYSIS. Executing the malware to see how it interacts with the host and its impact after infection. Reveals domain names, file path locations, created registry keys, IP addresses, additional files, installation files, DLLs, linked files.\nthe courseware recommends BOTH be performed for a detailed understanding."},{"deck":"Cloud Forensics","front":"Cloud Forensics Challenges — the six categories","back":"Source: NIST Cloud Computing Forensic Science Challenges.\nARCHITECTURE AND IDENTIFICATION — deletion in cloud, recovering overwritten data, interoperability among CSPs\nROLE MANAGEMENT — identifying account owner, fictitious identities, decoupling user credentials and physical location, authentication and access control\nSTANDARDS — testability/validation/scientific principles not addressed, lack of standard processes and models\nTRAINING — limited knowledge of logs and records, cloud training for investigators\nANTI-FORENSICS — anti-forensic techniques mislead the incident handling process\nINCIDENT FIRST RESPONDERS — competence and trustworthiness"},{"deck":"Cloud Forensics","front":"Logs in AWS — what each one answers","back":"CLOUDTRAIL — AWS API call history for the account, including calls via Management Console, CLI, SDKs. WHO did WHAT.\nS3 SERVER ACCESS LOGS — access requests to any S3 bucket including PUT, GET, DELETE. Fields: bucket owner, bucket name, name and IP of requester, request time and ID, Request-URI, response status.\nVPC FLOW LOGS — inbound and outbound IP traffic on network interfaces within a VPC. Source and destination IPs and ports, timestamp, action performed.\nELB ACCESS LOGS — requests made to the load balancer; used for TRAFFIC PATTERN ANALYSIS.\nAWS DATABASE LOGS — viewed via the Management Console; the tail log is refreshed by RDS every 5 SECONDS.\nCloudWatch (metrics/app logs) · GuardDuty (threat findings)."},{"deck":"Mobile Forensics","front":"Android File System — the six primary partitions","back":"/boot — boot image and kernel; device cannot boot without it; read-only unless rooted\n/system — the entire Android OS including GUI components and pre-installed applications\n/recovery — recovery image, backups, maintenance operations\n/data — app and USER data: photos, music, videos, contacts, settings, apps\n/cache — temporary files generated by both OS and applications\n/misc — miscellaneous settings: carrier details, hardware settings, configurations, bootloader state\nOptional extras: /sdcard (internal storage emulates this — user files and app saved files) and /sd-ext (external storage)."},{"deck":"Mobile Forensics","front":"iOS File System — key directories","back":"APFS since iOS 10.3, replacing HFS+. Optimised for SSD/flash; strong encryption, space sharing, cloning, snapshots.\n/Applications — all built-in and third-party apps\n/Library — system files and preferences\n/usr — user-related utilities and applications\n/var — VARIABLE data: logs and temporary files\n/tmp — temporary files that get CLEARED ON REBOOT\nRoot directory (/) branches into Applications, User data, System files."},{"deck":"Mobile Forensics","front":"Mobile Data Acquisition Methods (5)","back":"Cellular data acquisition · File system acquisition · Logical acquisition of device · Physical acquisition of device · Cloud data acquisition.\nBefore logical and physical acquisition, investigators must deal with PHONE LOCKING and ROOTING/JAILBREAKING.\nSelection depends on: time constraints for extraction · acquisition types supported by the device · required live data · recovery of deleted data · third-party application data · available tools."},{"deck":"Dark Web Forensics","front":"Tor Browser artifacts — registry and state file","back":"REGISTRY: HKEY_USERS\\<SID>\\SOFTWARE\\Mozilla\\Firefox\\Launcher — gives the PATH from which Tor Browser was executed.\nSTATE FILE: \\Tor Browser\\Browser\\TorBrowser\\Data\\Tor\\state — gives the LAST EXECUTION date and time. The state-file generation time is in LOCAL time; other times in the file are in UTC.\nAlso: Command Prompt, Prefetch files, and the places.sqlite file."},{"deck":"Email Forensics","front":"Outlook local files — .pst vs .ost","back":".PST (Personal Storage Table) — used by POP accounts to save mailbox information locally. Default: C:\\Users\\%USERNAME%\\Documents\\Outlook Files\n.OST (Offline Storage Table) — used by Microsoft Exchange, Office 365 and IMAP accounts. Default: C:\\Users\\%USERNAME%\\AppData\\Local\\Microsoft\\Outlook\nARCHIVE folder — default Outlook folder alongside Inbox/Drafts/Sent Items. Auto Archive location: File → Options → Advanced → AutoArchive Settings. Archived data is saved in .PST format."},{"deck":"Web Attack Forensics","front":"Indicators of a Web Attack","back":"Unavailability of website, web services or applications · alerts from WAF, SIEM, IDS · redirection of URLs to incorrect sites · web page defacements · unusually slow network performance · frequent rebooting of the server · anomalies in web server, application and database logs · database logs showing multiple errors in a short duration · suspicious activity in user accounts (new processes, users, jobs) · 400–500 error messages · abnormal behaviour such as popups and spam messages · changes in web application files · search engines flagging the site as insecure · complaints from customers · unusual outbound and inbound traffic · inappropriate login attempts from various geographical locations · too many requests for the same file."},{"deck":"Anti-forensics","front":"Anti-forensics Techniques — the 10","back":"1 Data/File Deletion · 2 Password Protection · 3 Steganography · 4 Data Hiding in File System Structures · 5 Trail Obfuscation · 6 Artifact Wiping · 7 Overwriting Data/Metadata · 8 Encryption · 9 Program Packers · 10 Minimizing Footprint.\nAnti-forensics (a.k.a. COUNTER FORENSICS) aims at complicating or preventing a proper forensic investigation process."},{"deck":"Anti-forensics","front":"File deletion — FAT vs NTFS","back":"FAT: the OS replaces the FIRST LETTER of the deleted filename with hex byte code E5h — a special tag indicating deletion. The cluster is marked unused but continues to hold the information until overwritten.\nNTFS: the OS marks the file entry UNALLOCATED in the MFT but does not delete the actual content. Clusters are marked free in $Bitmap (the record of all used and unused clusters). Recoverable while the space is not allocated to another file.\nNOTE: normal Delete → Recycle Bin. SHIFT+DELETE BYPASSES the Recycle Bin."},{"deck":"Anti-forensics","front":"SSD carving — TRIM enabled vs disabled","back":"TRIM ENABLED: when carving a forensic image acquired from a TRIM-enabled SSD it is IMPOSSIBLE to recover the deleted data. Autopsy will LIST deleted filenames but the data cannot be recovered.\nTRIM DISABLED: file carving CAN recover lost data from the drive.\nDuring acquisition the investigator should CHECK the status of the SSD's TRIM feature. Tools: Autopsy, EaseUS Data Recovery."},{"deck":"File Systems","front":"MBR structure — the three parts and their sizes","back":"MASTER BOOT CODE / BOOT STRAP — executable code that loads the OS into memory: 446 BYTES\nPARTITION TABLE — data on all hard disk partitions: 64 BYTES (describes only FOUR primary/physical partitions; all others are logical partitions linked to a primary)\nDISK SIGNATURE — at the END of the MBR, 2 BYTES, required by BIOS during booting\n446 + 64 + 2 = 512 bytes = one sector."},{"deck":"File Systems","front":"Master boot code — its four functions","back":"1. Examines the partition table to find the active partition\n2. Locates the first sector of the active partition\n3. Loads a boot sector copy from the active partition into memory\n4. Transfers control to the executable code in the boot sector"},{"deck":"File Systems","front":"Clusters — sizes and why slack exists","back":"A cluster is the SMALLEST ACCESSIBLE STORAGE UNIT on a hard disk, formed by COMBINING SECTORS. Also called allocation units.\nFixed size set by the file system and disk formatting — typically 512 BYTES to 4 KB or higher.\nCluster size varies from 4 to 64 SECTORS; in some cases 128 sectors.\nAny read or write consumes a minimum of one cluster. When a file smaller than the cluster is stored, the extra space is wasted — that is SLACK SPACE."},{"deck":"File Systems","front":"Windows Boot Process — BIOS-MBR chain","back":"BIOS → MBR (0000h:7c00h) → VBR (Volume Boot Sector) → NT Boot Sector → BOOTMGR.EXE (BmMain / BmLaunchBootEntry) → WINLOAD.EXE (PsLoadedModulesList / OslArchTransferToKernel) → NTOSKRNL.EXE Phase 0 (HalInitializeBios / KiInitializeKernel) → Phase 1 (HalInitSystem / ObInitSystem) → SMSS.EXE → WINLOGON.EXE → LSASS.EXE.\nSide branches: HAL.DLL, WIN32K.SYS, WINRESUME.EXE, BCD (Boot Configuration Data).\nWindows 10+ uses either BIOS-MBR or the newer UEFI-GPT method."},{"deck":"Web Attack Forensics","front":"Web Attack Investigation Methodology (8 steps)","back":"1 Conduct individual interviews to obtain information on the attack\n2 Locate the servers/devices involved, take them OFFLINE, and seize in a forensically sound manner\n3 Follow the process of forensic image acquisition and duplication\n4 Collect logs from web server, application server, database server, WAF, local system events, SIEM and IDS, plus application and server configuration files\n5 Use ENCRYPTION AND CHECKSUM to verify and protect the integrity of log files\n6 Analyze the WORKING COPIES of collected logs for suspicious entries and correlate to build a chain of events\n7 Trace the attacking IP to identify the perpetrator — generally very difficult as attackers use proxies and anonymizers\n8 Document every step; essential for legal proceedings"},{"deck":"Web Attack Forensics","front":"IIS log location and format","back":"%SystemDrive%\\inetpub\\logs\\LogFiles — ASCII TEXT-BASED log files on Windows Server OSes.\nRecords client IP address, username, date and time, request type, and target of operation.\nFields seen in W3C format: date, time, s-ip, cs-method, cs-uri-stem, cs-uri-query, s-port, cs-username, c-ip, cs(User-Agent), cs(Referer), sc-status, sc-substatus, sc-win32-status, time-taken.\nNOTE: the storage location may vary if the administrator has reconfigured it."},{"deck":"Web Attack Forensics","front":"Apache log locations by distribution","back":"ACCESS LOG (all HTTP requests processed; set by the CustomLog directive; common log format by default):\n  RHEL/CentOS/Fedora → /var/log/httpd/access_log\n  Debian/Ubuntu → /var/log/apache2/access.log\n  FreeBSD → /var/log/httpd-access.log\nERROR LOG (all errors during client request processing; set by the ErrorLog directive; named error.log on Windows, error_log on Unix):\n  RHEL/CentOS/Fedora → /var/log/httpd/error_log\n  Debian/Ubuntu → /var/log/apache2/error.log\n  FreeBSD → /var/log/httpd-error.log\nRead with grep, cat, gedit, vi."},{"deck":"Dark Web Forensics","front":"Working of Tor Browser — onion routing","back":"Tor Browser is based on Mozilla's FIREFOX. User data is encrypted in MULTIPLE LAYERS like an onion, then sent through different relays.\nOne layer is decrypted at EACH SUCCESSIVE RELAY. At the EXIT RELAY the final layer is removed and the data reaches the destination server.\nThe destination server perceives the EXIT RELAY as the origin of the data — which is why identifying the true origin is extremely difficult.\nChain: User/Origin → Entry Relay → Middle Relays → Exit Relay → Destination Server. Traffic between user and exit relay is ENCRYPTED; exit relay to destination is UNENCRYPTED.\nProvides access to .ONION websites; the hidden service protocol lets users host anonymously with .BIT domains, accessible only from the Tor network."},{"deck":"Dark Web Forensics","front":"Tor places.sqlite — location and tables","back":"\\Tor Browser\\Browser\\TorBrowser\\Data\\Browser\\profile.default\\places.sqlite\nStores bookmarked sites, browsing history, and visited websites.\nKey tables: moz_places (visited websites with an ID and the URL), moz_historyvisits, moz_bookmarks.\nTool named by the courseware: SQLite Database Recovery."},{"deck":"File Systems","front":"MBR — the terminology trap","back":"the courseware's MBR TABLE (Module 03):\n0x0000 Code Area — 440 bytes (max. 446)\n0x01B8 DISK SIGNATURE (Optional) — 4 BYTES ← the 32-bit disk signature\n0x01BC usually nulls 0x0000 — 2 bytes\n0x01BE Table of Primary Partitions (four 16-byte entries) — 64 bytes\n0x01FE MBR SIGNATURE 0xAA55 (55H/AAH) — 2 BYTES ← the 'signature word'\nTotal: 446 + 64 + 2 = 512\n\nWARNING: the PROSE on the next page calls the 2-byte end marker the 'disk signature'. Exam keys follow the TABLE — the third MBR component is the SIGNATURE WORD."},{"deck":"Malware Forensics","front":"System Baselining vs Host Integrity Monitoring","back":"SYSTEM BASELINING — snapshot the baseline state BEFORE execution, run the sample, take a SECOND snapshot, COMPARE the two to detect changes to file systems and registry keys. Tool: WhatChanged Portable.\n\nHOST INTEGRITY MONITORING — execute the malware and observe operations IN REAL TIME to understand intent and functionality. Two ways:\n  · System Behavior Analysis — monitoring the CHANGES in OS resources upon execution: registry artifacts, processes, services and startup programs, event logs, API calls, device drivers, installation, system calls and scheduled tasks, files and folders\n  · Network Behavior Analysis — IP addresses, connected ports, DNS entries, browser activity\n\n⚠ EXAM KEY WARNING: 'What do you call the process of studying the changes that have taken place across a system or a machine after a series of actions or incidents?' → the OFFICIAL key answers HOST INTEGRITY MONITORING, not System Baselining. The wording reads like baselining; the key says otherwise. Go with the key."},{"deck":"Legacy Exam Trivia","front":"Which organization coordinates computer crime investigations throughout the US?","back":"NATIONAL INFRASTRUCTURE PROTECTION CENTER (NIPC) — the expected exam answer.\nNot: Internet Fraud Complaint Center (intake of fraud complaints, now IC3) · U.S. Secret Service (one agency among several) · CERT Coordination Center (vulnerability research and advisories, Carnegie Mellon — not law enforcement).\nREAL WORLD: NIPC was disbanded in 2003 and absorbed into DHS. Answer NIPC anyway — the item bank is old."},{"deck":"Legacy Exam Trivia","front":"Ping of Death — which header field?","back":"The IP HEADER FIELD. ICMP is only the CARRIER (ping = ICMP echo request); the exploit is in IP-level fragmentation and reassembly, where reassembled fragments exceed the 65,535-byte maximum and overflow the buffer.\nDo not answer ICMP — that is the trap."},{"deck":"Legacy Exam Trivia","front":"What TCP/UDP port does netstat use?","back":"PORT 15 — a legacy IANA assignment for a remote netstat service.\nDistractors: port 7 = Echo · port 23 = Telnet · port 69 = TFTP.\nModern netstat is a local diagnostic utility and listens on nothing. Pure legacy trivia — memorise it."},{"deck":"Legacy Exam Trivia","front":"Why note all cable connections before seizing a computer?","back":"IN CASE OTHER DEVICES WERE CONNECTED.\nDistractors are deliberately close: 'to know what outside connections existed' (too narrow — network only) · 'to know what peripheral devices exist' (describes the result, not the reason) · 'to know what hardware existed' (too generic).\nThe point is not overlooking an attached evidence device."},{"deck":"Legacy Exam Trivia","front":"Standard procedure re: system date and time","back":"WITH THE HARD DRIVE IN THE SUSPECT PC, CHECK THE DATE AND TIME IN THE SYSTEM'S CMOS.\nCMOS/BIOS clocks drift or are altered; documenting the offset at the scene lets you correct every timestamp recovered later. The FAT holds no system clock; RAM is volatile; and the drive must still be installed because this is done as part of initial scene documentation before removal."},{"deck":"Legacy Exam Trivia","front":"How do you verify a drive-wiping utility was used?","back":"LOOK FOR DISTINCT REPEATING PATTERNS ON THE HARD DRIVE AT THE BIT LEVEL.\nWipers overwrite with uniform patterns — 0x00, 0xFF, alternating 0xAA/0x55, or DoD 5220.22-M multi-pass patterns. Ordinary deleted data leaves fragmented, varied residue, not uniform overwrites.\nNot: checking installed programs (the registry hive was already checked) · documenting suspicion without evidence · installing wipers to export 'previous run reports'."},{"deck":"Legacy Exam Trivia","front":"Most efficient acquisition from four 30 TB SANs","back":"CREATE A SPARSE DATA COPY of the relevant folder or file.\nCue: whenever a question stresses VERY LARGE storage (SAN/NAS, multi-TB arrays) and asks for the MOST EFFICIENT method, the expected answer is sparse/selective acquisition — not a bit-stream disk-to-image or disk-to-disk, which are the gold standard but impractical at that scale."},{"deck":"Legacy Exam Trivia","front":"Which phase is First Response part of?","back":"THE INVESTIGATION PHASE.\nthe courseware's three phases: PRE-INVESTIGATION (setting up the lab, building the toolkit, forming the team, obtaining authorization) → INVESTIGATION (FIRST RESPONSE, search and seizure, evidence acquisition, preservation, examination, analysis) → POST-INVESTIGATION (documentation, reporting, expert testimony).\nIt FEELS preliminary, which is exactly why it is tested. It is not pre-investigation."},{"deck":"Legacy Exam Trivia","front":"Recycle Bin locations by Windows version","back":"Windows XP (NTFS) → Drive:\\RECYCLER\nFAT32 → Drive:\\RECYCLED\nWindows Vista / 7 and later (NTFS) → Drive:\\$Recycle.Bin\nLegacy record file = INFO2 (one file, many records). Vista+ = per-file $I (path, deletion time, size) and $R (content).\nFOUR questions in the 150-question bank test these paths. Watch for the misspelled distractor 'REYCLED'."},{"deck":"Legacy Exam Trivia","front":"US laws and standards — the set that gets tested","back":"18 USC §1030 = fraud and related activity in connection with COMPUTERS (§1029 = access devices)\nSOX 2002 = protects investors from fraudulent corporate accounting\nHIPAA = patient health records\nCAN-SPAM = commercial email: no misleading headers, must offer opt-out, must give physical postal address\nFourth Amendment = unlawful search and seizure\nFRE 1001 definitions · 1002 REQUIREMENT OF ORIGINAL · 1003 duplicates · 1004 other evidence of contents\nISO/IEC 17025 = forensic LAB accreditation (27037 = evidence handling)\nSWGDE = quality systems; Standards and Criteria 1.5 = appropriate and effective hardware/software"},{"deck":"Legacy Exam Trivia","front":"Windows Event IDs — the extended set","back":"4624 successful logon · 4625 failed logon · 4634/4647 logoff · 4648 explicit credentials · 4657 REGISTRY VALUE MODIFIED · 4672 special privileges · 4688/4689 process create/terminate · 4720/4726 account create/delete · 1102 audit log cleared · 4104/4103 PowerShell script block / module logging · 7040 service start-type changed.\nLog types: SYSTEM = device drivers and hardware · SECURITY = logons and object access · APPLICATION = app events · FORWARDED EVENTS = collected from other machines."},{"deck":"Legacy Exam Trivia","front":"Mobile identifiers — IMEI, ICCID, PUK","back":"IMEI = 15 digits; first EIGHT = Type Allocation Code (TAC) giving model and origin.\nICCID = SIM serial; first TWO digits = industry identifier (89 = telecom), next TWO = country code.\nIMSI = subscriber identity on the SIM.\nThree failed PIN attempts BLOCK the SIM — the network operator supplies the PUK (Personal Unlock Number) to reset it.\nCDRs hold METADATA (sequence number, duration, numbers, timestamps, cell site) — never call content or language."},{"deck":"Legacy Exam Trivia","front":"Anti-forensic data-hiding tools (MAFIA set)","back":"Slacker → hides data in SLACK space\nRuneFS → hides data in BAD BLOCKS\nWaffenFS → hides data in the ext3 JOURNAL\nFragFS → hides data in the NTFS $MFT\nTimestomp → alters timestamps (analyzeMFT DETECTS it)\nPackers → compress/encrypt executables to evade reverse-engineering and scanning"},{"deck":"Legacy Exam Trivia","front":"Tool-to-task pairs from the official bank","back":"Devcon → document connected USB devices · pmdump.exe → dump process memory without stopping the process · PWdump7 → extract users/passwords/hashes from SAM and SYSTEM · Winrtgen → GENERATE rainbow tables · Stream Detector → detect and extract NTFS alternate data streams · analyzeMFT → detect timestomping · R-Studio → damaged MBR/partition and deleted-partition recovery · FileSalvage → Mac file recovery · MZCacheView → Mozilla browser cache · Helix → acquire volatile data (Windows) · OllyDbg → disassembly · oleid.py → static triage of MS Office documents · ModSecurity → web application firewall · ApexSQL Audit → MS SQL login sessions and transactions · myisamlog → MyISAM log recovery · ADB → Android acquisition · jv16 → registry analysis/monitoring · CHKDSK → repair logical file system errors."},{"deck":"Legacy Exam Trivia","front":"Database forensics essentials","back":"MS SQL: MDF = primary data file, the STARTING POINT storing user data and database objects · NDF = secondary · LDF = transaction log.\nSelect * from ::fn_dblog(NULL, NULL) — NULLs mean start and end points for the log file are NOT specified, so the whole active log returns.\nsqlcmd -S <name> — the -S value is the SQL SERVER name.\nMySQL: <hostname>-bin.nnnnnn = BINARY log recording changes · myisamlog recovers MyISAM logs · ibdata1 = InnoDB tablespace."},{"deck":"Legacy Exam Trivia","front":"Web attack signatures in logs","back":"XSS → <SCRIPT> tags, document.cookie exfiltration. Regex parts: %3C = '<' opening, %3E = '>' closing, %2F = '/' slash.\nSQL injection → SQL keywords such as UNION SELECT in the query string.\nDirectory traversal → ../ or encoded %c0%af sequences reaching outside the web root.\nSession fixation → attacker SETS the session ID in advance and shares the URL (hijacking STEALS it afterwards).\nApache COMMON log = host, identity, user, timestamp, request, status, size. COMBINED adds referrer and user-agent.\nApache ERROR log = [date] [error] [client IP] message."},{"deck":"Legacy Exam Trivia","front":"Cloud and Azure specifics from the official bank","back":"SLA = the document defining CSP/client roles and responsibilities — read it first.\nAzure PAGE blob = the blob type for VHD files and OS-disk backups (block blob is the tempting default).\nAzure CLI = copy an OS disk snapshot to a storage account in another region.\nAfter creating a vhd backup in another region, DELETE the snapshot from the source resource group.\nAWS: after snapshotting the EBS volume and collecting evidence, TERMINATE the infected instance after backup."},{"deck":"Legacy Exam Trivia","front":"IoT and mobile architecture terms","back":"IoT layers: EDGE TECHNOLOGY (sensors, RFID tags, devices — data collection) · Access gateway · Middleware · Application.\nSybil attack = many FORGED IDENTITIES creating illusory traffic congestion.\nOWASP IoT 'lack of secure update mechanism' = no firmware validation, no secure delivery, no anti-rollback.\nAndroid: Zygote = pre-warmed parent process giving low memory use and fast start-up · SGL = 2D, OpenGL/ES = 3D graphics.\niOS layers: Cocoa Touch (app frameworks, topmost) · Media services · Core services · Core OS."}];
